\details{
\subsection{Layouts}{

\strong{Long} files carry one row per sample, with every column other than the
sample column (and any \code{metadataCols}) treated as a marker:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{Sample,  D1S1656, DYS391, AMEL
Line1,   "12,14", 12,     X
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Wide} files carry one row per sample/marker pair, with the alleles spread
across several columns. Any column whose name contains \code{Allele} is collected;
size and height columns are ignored:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{Sample, Marker,  Allele 1, Size 1, Allele 2
Line1,  D3S1358, 16,       128.29, 18
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

With \code{format = "auto"} a file is read as wide when it has a \code{markerCol}
column \emph{and} at least one column containing \code{Allele}, and long otherwise.
}

\subsection{Combining files}{

Files may mix layouts and formats. Markers are unioned across files, with
samples missing a marker recorded as untyped rather than as an empty allele.
Sample names must be unique across all files.
}

\subsection{Divergences from the Python package}{

\code{metadataCols} are held in \code{\link[=sampleData]{sampleData()}} rather than alongside the markers.
\code{strprofiler} (from 0.5.0) carries them in the profile and skips them at scoring and
mixing time, so a custom metadata column has to be declared to each of those
functions rather than once at ingest. (Through 0.4.2 it had no such argument,
and two samples sharing a \code{Center} of \code{"JAX"} scored as sharing a marker.)

Row order follows the input files. \code{strprofiler} returns wide-format samples
in sorted order because it groups with \code{pandas}.

All columns are read as text, so alleles are never coerced to numbers and
back. This removes a class of bug that \code{strprofiler} patched twice through
0.4.2 (alleles ending in zero being truncated, for example \code{10} becoming \code{1});
since 0.5.0 it instead parses every call and renders it back.
}
